WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.290 --> 00:00:06.700
For 146 years, the Barnum and Bailey Circus
was known for being the greatest show on earth.

00:00:06.700 --> 00:00:12.090
None of it would have been possible if it
were not for an ambitious entrepreneur named

00:00:12.090 --> 00:00:17.840
PT Barnum. While his life and career were
sometimes the source of controversy, he truly

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:23.020
was a brilliant businessman, and many of his
ideas forever changed the entertainment industry

00:00:23.020 --> 00:00:31.810
as we know it today.
Early Life

00:00:31.810 --> 00:00:41.550
Phineas Taylor Barnum was born in 1810 in
Bethel, Connecticut. His parents, Philo Barnum

00:00:41.550 --> 00:00:46.361
and Irene Taylor were farmers who rented out
rooms in their home for extra income. His

00:00:46.361 --> 00:00:51.941
grandfather was a well-respected judge in
the town. On the day of his baptism, PT Barnum

00:00:51.941 --> 00:00:58.280
inherited a 4.7-acre piece of land from his
grandfather called “Ivy Island”. His parents

00:00:58.280 --> 00:01:03.360
were poor, but as a child, he grew up believing
that he was the richest kid in town, because

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:08.390
he thought that he inherited a private island
somewhere that he could build a house on some

00:01:08.390 --> 00:01:13.430
day. When he was 10 years old, his parents
finally took him on a trip to visit Ivy Island,

00:01:13.430 --> 00:01:18.970
where he discovered that it was virtually
worthless. The island was muddy land in the

00:01:18.970 --> 00:01:23.490
middle of a swamp, and it was filled with
poisonous snakes, ticks, and poison ivy. He

00:01:23.490 --> 00:01:28.630
never did anything with the land. Today, it
now part of a nature reserve called the Bethel

00:01:28.630 --> 00:01:30.060
Land Trust.

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:35.750
This was clearly disappointing, and it left
him yearning for a reality where he truly

00:01:35.750 --> 00:01:39.830
would be rich enough to buy a private island
some day. But he wasn’t interested in the

00:01:39.830 --> 00:01:44.369
traditional ways of making money. By his own
admission, he had the reputation of being

00:01:44.369 --> 00:01:50.331
“the laziest boy in town”, because he
hated doing farm work and manual labor. Instead

00:01:50.331 --> 00:01:54.930
of forcing Phineas into caring for the family
farm, his father helped get him a job as a

00:01:54.930 --> 00:02:00.930
clerk in the town grocery store. This was
a perfect fit, and Phineas grew to love the

00:02:00.930 --> 00:02:06.080
idea of running his own business. When he
was only 15 years old, his father died, and

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:11.310
he was now the man of the family. This motivated
him even more to make as much money as he

00:02:11.310 --> 00:02:17.019
possibly could. He was possessed by the entrepreneurial
spirit, and he began trying different business

00:02:17.019 --> 00:02:18.959
ventures right away.

00:02:18.959 --> 00:02:23.150
His most successful business as a teenager
was running a lottery. While working in the

00:02:23.150 --> 00:02:28.150
grocery store, he had an endless supply of
glass bottles and tin advertising trays that

00:02:28.150 --> 00:02:33.799
he was expected to throw away. He knew that
these items would be useful in people’s

00:02:33.799 --> 00:02:39.219
homes, and he wanted to recycle them in some
way. But people were not interested in buying

00:02:39.219 --> 00:02:44.930
them second-hand. He started selling tickets
for a lottery, instead. He promised that half

00:02:44.930 --> 00:02:49.569
of the tickets sold would be guaranteed to
win something. There were a few big cash prizes,

00:02:49.569 --> 00:02:54.480
and consolation prizes were those items he
got for free. He sold 1,000 tickets in just

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:58.609
the first week, and he continued to run the
lottery over and over again.

00:02:58.609 --> 00:03:02.590
When he was just 16 years old, he used all
of the money he earned from these lotteries

00:03:02.590 --> 00:03:08.099
to move to Brooklyn, New York to open his
own grocery store. Unfortunately, he caught

00:03:08.099 --> 00:03:12.419
smallpox, and had to move back to Connecticut
so his mother could help nurse him back to

00:03:12.419 --> 00:03:17.169
health. After recovering from his illness,
he knew he needed to try again. When he was

00:03:17.169 --> 00:03:22.279
18, he opened up a confectionary shop in his
home town that sold fruit and candy. He was

00:03:22.279 --> 00:03:27.060
able to run more lotteries in his own store.
He married his first wife, Charity Hallett,

00:03:27.060 --> 00:03:32.139
when he was 19 years old, and she was 21.
They would eventually go on to have four daughters

00:03:32.139 --> 00:03:37.540
together. While he was never overly romantic,
he did write that his wife was a “treasure”,

00:03:37.540 --> 00:03:40.669
and that “Without Charity, I am nothing.”

00:03:40.669 --> 00:03:45.010
When he was 21, he wrote several passionate
articles about politics and sent them in to

00:03:45.010 --> 00:03:49.529
the local newspaper, but they were all rejected.
He decided that if no one was going to publish

00:03:49.529 --> 00:03:54.769
his work, he would self-publish his own paper,
called The Herald Freedom. He had enough money

00:03:54.769 --> 00:03:59.340
to buy his own printing press, and handed
out the newspapers in town. In one of his

00:03:59.340 --> 00:04:03.370
articles, he exposed the corrupt leaders of
the town’s local church. The men sued him

00:04:03.370 --> 00:04:07.769
for libel, since he had no proof to back up
his claims in court. He ended up paying a

00:04:07.769 --> 00:04:13.430
$100 fine and spent 60 days in jail. When
he was released, he became a local hero, and

00:04:13.430 --> 00:04:19.310
a leader of the liberal movement. Everyone
loved him in Bridgeport, but he decided that

00:04:19.310 --> 00:04:25.060
he achieved all he could possibly do in Connecticut.
He wanted to move to New York City, where

00:04:25.060 --> 00:04:35.080
he knew he would have so many more opportunities
to earn his fortune.

00:04:35.080 --> 00:04:37.810
The First Taste Of Show Business
PT and Charity Barnum bought a boarding house

00:04:37.810 --> 00:04:42.780
in New York City so they could earn some income
by renting out their extra rooms, just like

00:04:42.780 --> 00:04:47.120
his parents had done back in Connecticut.
He used his knowledge from working in retail

00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:52.000
to have partial ownership of a grocery store
without having to do any of the work, and

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:56.670
continued to run his weekly lotteries. He
set himself up in a pretty great financial

00:04:56.670 --> 00:05:01.730
situation, and he was still in his early 20’s.
His family’s basic needs of food and housing

00:05:01.730 --> 00:05:07.830
were met, but his sights were on bigger and
better ways to make as much money as he possibly

00:05:07.830 --> 00:05:08.830
could.

00:05:08.830 --> 00:05:13.790
When he was 25 years old, he met a man who
told him about Joice Heth, who was an elderly

00:05:13.790 --> 00:05:18.390
African American slave woman. She claimed
to be George Washington’s nanny, and would

00:05:18.390 --> 00:05:23.630
go on and on with her stories of their time
together. If this was true, it would have

00:05:23.630 --> 00:05:29.890
made her 161 years old. Barnum was able to
pay her owner $1,000 in order to put her on

00:05:29.890 --> 00:05:35.340
display. She looked so incredibly old, that
people actually believed that she may have

00:05:35.340 --> 00:05:42.110
truly been 161. People came from all over
New York City to see her. She died after only

00:05:42.110 --> 00:05:46.990
one year of touring. There was a public autopsy
that revealed she was only about 80 years

00:05:46.990 --> 00:05:52.560
old, but people were not angry about the deception.
While it was a “humbug”, people still

00:05:52.560 --> 00:05:58.090
enjoyed the spectacle. This gave PT Barnum
a preview of the showman experience. If he

00:05:58.090 --> 00:06:02.990
could find even more fantastic and interesting
attractions, he didn’t have to do any of

00:06:02.990 --> 00:06:09.140
the hard manual labor he dreaded so much.
He could just hire talented people to perform,

00:06:09.140 --> 00:06:11.640
and collect the money from ticket sales.

00:06:11.640 --> 00:06:16.750
PT Barnum tried to sell tickets to acts with
performers who specialized in juggling and

00:06:16.750 --> 00:06:21.840
plate spinning, but most of those shows did
not do well, because people had seen these

00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:27.250
acts before. He realized that he had gotten
very lucky with having Joice Heth as his very

00:06:27.250 --> 00:06:32.480
first attraction. People really wanted to
pay for something they had never seen, but

00:06:32.480 --> 00:06:37.010
he had a difficult time figuring out what
to look for without having any experience

00:06:37.010 --> 00:06:41.490
in the entertainment industry. When he was
26, he temporarily left behind his wife and

00:06:41.490 --> 00:06:46.090
children to join a traveling circus in order
to educate himself on everything he needed

00:06:46.090 --> 00:06:48.180
to know about show business.

00:06:48.180 --> 00:06:52.900
The owner of the circus was a man named Aaron
Turner, and Barnum told him that he was eager

00:06:52.900 --> 00:06:57.440
to learn more about the business. Turner decided
to give him a first-hand experience with getting

00:06:57.440 --> 00:07:02.000
a crowd’s attention. The day before their
opening show, Turner pointed at PT Barnum

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:06.280
when he was standing in front of a crowd,
claiming that Barnum was Reverend Ephraim

00:07:06.280 --> 00:07:11.190
Avery, who was well-known in the newspapers
for being acquitted of murder. Everyone knew

00:07:11.190 --> 00:07:16.310
that Avery was truly guilty, and they were
all talking about him. Not many people knew

00:07:16.310 --> 00:07:19.940
what he actually looked like, but they all
passionately hated this man.

00:07:19.940 --> 00:07:24.730
Of course, people believed Turner’s claim,
and they began chasing after Barnum, who started

00:07:24.730 --> 00:07:28.510
running away. The angry mob got bigger and
bigger, and they were ready to take justice

00:07:28.510 --> 00:07:34.410
into their own hands by lynching the “murderer”.
At the last moment, Aaron Turner shouted that

00:07:34.410 --> 00:07:38.630
they were circus performers, and he did it
as a practical joke. Even though this could

00:07:38.630 --> 00:07:43.720
have been seen as “bad publicity”, it
worked. Everyone in the town was talking about

00:07:43.720 --> 00:07:48.650
the angry mob, and wanted to know what kind
of crazy men would pull such dangerous jokes

00:07:48.650 --> 00:07:52.620
on each other. They bought tickets to the
show the next day.

00:07:52.620 --> 00:07:57.450
After six months with the circus, Barnum started
his own small troupe of traveling performers

00:07:57.450 --> 00:08:03.130
called, “Barnum's Grand Scientific and Musical
Theater”. He even bought a steam boat to

00:08:03.130 --> 00:08:07.560
give tours and musical performances along
the rivers in the south. But after about a

00:08:07.560 --> 00:08:12.400
year of living on the road, he became very
homesick. He missed his wife and kids, and

00:08:12.400 --> 00:08:17.330
he wanted stability. He returned to New York,
hoping to find a way to display curiosities

00:08:17.330 --> 00:08:21.540
and performances without traveling from place
to place.

00:08:21.540 --> 00:08:29.820
The American Museum
In 1841, Barnum purchased the Scudder’s

00:08:29.820 --> 00:08:34.680
American Museum and transformed it to be a
much more appealing place to visit. He added

00:08:34.680 --> 00:08:39.469
flags to the outside, and created an elaborate
rooftop garden, where a hot air balloon gave

00:08:39.469 --> 00:08:45.129
people rides on a daily basis. Inside, he
had a menagerie of exotic animals, magicians,

00:08:45.129 --> 00:08:48.129
and miniaturized recreations of famous battles.

00:08:48.129 --> 00:08:53.861
PT Barnum was a genius when it came to getting
more people to come to his attractions. When

00:08:53.861 --> 00:08:58.959
the American Museum first opened, he wasn’t
selling as many tickets as he had hoped. So,

00:08:58.959 --> 00:09:03.379
he found an unemployed man, and paid him to
run through a cycle of various movements.

00:09:03.379 --> 00:09:07.150
He would pick up a brick on the street, carry
it to the door, show his ticket to the museum,

00:09:07.150 --> 00:09:11.790
and walk inside. Then, he would walk back
out again, place the brick down, and pick

00:09:11.790 --> 00:09:16.019
up another. He continued to do this every
single day, to the point where people noticed

00:09:16.019 --> 00:09:21.160
this ritual on their way to and from work.
Some would even stop to watch this man, and

00:09:21.160 --> 00:09:25.670
they asked what he was doing. It was enough
to get people curious enough to buy a ticket

00:09:25.670 --> 00:09:29.790
for themselves, so they could see inside.

00:09:29.790 --> 00:09:34.009
As time went on, Barnum used the money he
made from ticket sales to improve the quality

00:09:34.009 --> 00:09:39.639
of his displays. He added one of his most
famous hoaxes, The Fiji Mermaid. He claimed

00:09:39.639 --> 00:09:43.339
that it was the body of a mermaid that had
been caught off the coast of Fiji, but it

00:09:43.339 --> 00:09:49.360
was really the skeleton of a monkey sewn onto
a fishtail. One of his most popular live exhibits

00:09:49.360 --> 00:09:53.800
was known as the “Freak Show”. While some
people disapprove of the fact that he exploited

00:09:53.800 --> 00:09:58.279
people with physical abnormalities like bearded
ladies and people with extra limbs, he was

00:09:58.279 --> 00:10:02.370
giving many of these people work when they
would have otherwise never found a job out

00:10:02.370 --> 00:10:06.490
in the real world. He also paid his employees
very well, and they were all treated like

00:10:06.490 --> 00:10:11.240
members of the family. Every single one of
the members of the so-called Freak Shows became

00:10:11.240 --> 00:10:16.620
incredibly rich, and all they had to do was
stand on display. Some of them chose to retire

00:10:16.620 --> 00:10:22.519
after just a few years of working, and they
went on to get married and have normal lives.

00:10:22.519 --> 00:10:26.800
When Barnum was 33 years old, he had been
running his American Museum for a few years,

00:10:26.800 --> 00:10:31.889
and had found some success. But he was always
looking out for the next big thing. He met

00:10:31.889 --> 00:10:37.050
Charles Stratton, who was one of his distant
cousins. Charles was only 4 years old when

00:10:37.050 --> 00:10:42.480
they met. He was a proportionate dwarf, standing
only 25 inches tall. He would stay the size

00:10:42.480 --> 00:10:47.079
of a baby for the rest of his life. Charles’
father was a carpenter, so their family did

00:10:47.079 --> 00:10:51.199
not have a lot of money. They were afraid
for their son’s future. Not many dwarves

00:10:51.199 --> 00:10:56.089
could find work outside of the entertainment
industry, and they were never the star of

00:10:56.089 --> 00:11:02.279
the show. They were always on the sidelines,
used as the butt of a joke. PT Barnum agreed

00:11:02.279 --> 00:11:06.950
to be his cousin’s manager, and he spent
months teaching him how to sing, dance, and

00:11:06.950 --> 00:11:11.329
act. He was not sure how well the audiences
would enjoy the show, so at first, he paid

00:11:11.329 --> 00:11:16.689
the boy’s parents $3 a week, which is the
modern-day equivalent of $70. His parents

00:11:16.689 --> 00:11:21.379
agreed, and they were happy that their young
son could earn any money at all.

00:11:21.379 --> 00:11:26.100
PT Barnum’s strategy with Charles’ performance
was to make him the polar opposite of what

00:11:26.100 --> 00:11:31.520
people expected from a dwarf. He gave the
boy the stage name of “Tom Thumb”, after

00:11:31.520 --> 00:11:36.060
the well-loved English fairy tale. He also
claimed that he was already 10 years old instead

00:11:36.060 --> 00:11:40.800
of 5, which made his small stature even more
incredible. Thankfully, Charles was very smart

00:11:40.800 --> 00:11:47.639
child, and he picked up on how to talk with
the a bigger vocabulary. Barnum advertised

00:11:47.639 --> 00:11:52.829
Tom Thumb as a “tiny gentleman”, dressing
him in tailor-made suits. He was able to memorize

00:11:52.829 --> 00:11:57.839
complicated lines, and performed roles of
dignified figures in history, like Napoleon

00:11:57.839 --> 00:12:00.420
Bonaparte. It worked.

00:12:00.420 --> 00:12:04.420
He traveled all over the United States and
Europe, becoming one of the world’s first

00:12:04.420 --> 00:12:10.120
international celebrities. Even Queen Victoria
was a fan of little Tom Thumb. As time went

00:12:10.120 --> 00:12:15.509
on, PT Barnum hired other proportionate dwarves
to join his American Museum. This is where

00:12:15.509 --> 00:12:20.149
Charles Stratton met his future wife, Lavinia
Warren. Barnum threw them a wedding ceremony

00:12:20.149 --> 00:12:25.259
that became front-page news. President Abraham
Lincoln was also a huge fan, and threw them

00:12:25.259 --> 00:12:30.509
a honeymoon party at the White House. Stratton
made so much money through performing, he

00:12:30.509 --> 00:12:35.610
could have retired at a very young age. But
he genuinely enjoyed it so much, he continued

00:12:35.610 --> 00:12:38.829
until he died at 45 years old.

00:12:38.829 --> 00:12:43.410
The museum became so popular, that people
would buy one ticket and stay there all day,

00:12:43.410 --> 00:12:47.019
even if they got to the end of the tour. It
would get so crowded, that Barnum had to figure

00:12:47.019 --> 00:12:50.971
out a way to trick people into leaving. He
put up a sign that said, “This way to the

00:12:50.971 --> 00:12:55.730
Egress”. People assumed that it was another
attraction, but really, “egress” is just

00:12:55.730 --> 00:13:00.149
a fancy word for “exit”. Once they left
the building, they could no longer open the

00:13:00.149 --> 00:13:03.990
door, and if they wanted to come back inside,
they needed to stand in line and buy another

00:13:03.990 --> 00:13:10.709
ticket.
Iranistan, and The Music Business

00:13:10.709 --> 00:13:16.110
After having so much success with his American
Museum, Barnum was able to build a massive

00:13:16.110 --> 00:13:20.399
palace in Connecticut for his family to live.
He called the home Iranistan, because of its

00:13:20.399 --> 00:13:24.679
middle-eastern inspiration. He modeled the
building after the Brighton Royal Pavilion

00:13:24.679 --> 00:13:29.699
in England, which was the summer home of George,
Prince of Wales. He was able to host events

00:13:29.699 --> 00:13:34.529
and bring in famous guest from around the
world, like the author Mark Twain. He used

00:13:34.529 --> 00:13:40.139
the large halls for musical performances.
He believed that by building such a huge castle,

00:13:40.139 --> 00:13:45.430
famous musicians would be more likely to perform
at his venues. It worked.

00:13:45.430 --> 00:13:50.310
In 1850, PT Barnum tried his luck at finding
musical talent from overseas. He convinced

00:13:50.310 --> 00:13:55.350
a Swedish opera singer named Jenny Lind to
come to New York to perform. According to

00:13:55.350 --> 00:13:59.889
his autobiography, Jenny Lind only agreed
to travel to the United States because she

00:13:59.889 --> 00:14:05.360
saw the engraving of Iranistan on the invitation
letter, and she wanted to visit this intriguing

00:14:05.360 --> 00:14:10.379
palace. He had never heard her sing, but he
learned of her reputation when visiting Europe.

00:14:10.379 --> 00:14:16.079
Barnum was taking a huge risk by hiring Jenny
Lind, especially since he had no experience

00:14:16.079 --> 00:14:21.079
in the music industry. He even paid her in
advance to travel to The United States. But

00:14:21.079 --> 00:14:26.269
Barnum used his marketing genius to hype up
her arrival so much, Americans believed she

00:14:26.269 --> 00:14:30.769
was one of the greatest singers in the world,
and that they would be foolish to miss her

00:14:30.769 --> 00:14:35.809
limited-time performance. He created posters
that dubbed her “The Swedish Nightingale”.

00:14:35.809 --> 00:14:40.519
Her first performance was held in the Castle
Garden, and tickets were sold out.

00:14:40.519 --> 00:14:46.649
The shows were so popular, Jenny Lind performed
with Barnum 93 times over the course of five

00:14:46.649 --> 00:14:51.249
months. This new-found fame gave her enough
momentum to hire a new manager and go on a

00:14:51.249 --> 00:14:57.759
world tour. Barnum was taking a cut of the
ticket sales, of course. Jenny Lind earned

00:14:57.759 --> 00:15:03.319
$350,000 while she was working with Barnum
for those few months, and she went on to make

00:15:03.319 --> 00:15:10.329
more during her tour. Barnum received $500,000,
which is the modern-day equivalent of nearly

00:15:10.329 --> 00:15:12.329
$15 million.

00:15:12.329 --> 00:15:18.199
Tragically, in 1857, men who were hired to
do repairs on Iranistan while Barnum was away

00:15:18.199 --> 00:15:22.420
in New York left a pipe burning when they
left for the day, and it caused a fire that

00:15:22.420 --> 00:15:28.889
destroyed everything. His insurance policy
only paid out $28,000, even though it cost

00:15:28.889 --> 00:15:34.720
over $150,000 to build the mansion. And then,
in 1865, The American Museum burned down,

00:15:34.720 --> 00:15:39.079
as well. He rebuilt the museum, but after
only being open a couple years, it burned

00:15:39.079 --> 00:15:44.870
down a second time. Barnum said that the fires
“burned to the ground all of his life’s

00:15:44.870 --> 00:15:45.870
accomplishments”.

00:15:45.870 --> 00:15:53.829
The Barnum and Bailey Circus
At 60 years old, PT Barnum’s life was a

00:15:53.829 --> 00:15:58.519
roller coaster of success and failure. He
was at an age where most men would decide

00:15:58.519 --> 00:16:03.290
to cut their losses and retire. But one of
the many things that made PT Barnum such a

00:16:03.290 --> 00:16:08.209
success was that he didn’t let his failures
stop him from moving forward. His children

00:16:08.209 --> 00:16:12.860
were all grown up, and his wife Charity had
passed away. He decided that it was the right

00:16:12.860 --> 00:16:18.129
time in his life to start a traveling circus.
He was the very first circus owner to buy

00:16:18.129 --> 00:16:23.139
his own train to move the performers and equipment
from place to place. He paid his workers well,

00:16:23.139 --> 00:16:28.589
and he made sure to use bright, colorful,
elaborate tents and equipment to attract crowds.

00:16:28.589 --> 00:16:33.540
His first traveling show was called “P.
T. Barnum's Grand Traveling Museum, Menagerie,

00:16:33.540 --> 00:16:38.499
Caravan &amp; Hippodrome.” This had many of
the same attractions he once had in his American

00:16:38.499 --> 00:16:44.480
Museum, only on a grander scale. People outside
of New York City were able to see his freak

00:16:44.480 --> 00:16:48.829
show, animal menageries, and impressive acrobatic
talent that he had collected over the years

00:16:48.829 --> 00:16:51.519
of networking and making friends in show business.

00:16:51.519 --> 00:16:57.470
In 1881, when he was 71 years old, he joined
up with another circus owned by James Bailey,

00:16:57.470 --> 00:17:03.430
and their combined acts became the famous
Barnum &amp; Bailey Greatest Show On Earth. They

00:17:03.430 --> 00:17:07.360
were the first “three ring circus”- meaning
that they would have three different acts

00:17:07.360 --> 00:17:11.750
going on at the same time. One of his biggest
attractions at the Barnum &amp; Bailey Circus

00:17:11.750 --> 00:17:16.690
was Jumbo the Elephant. He was one of the
most popular animals for children in London,

00:17:16.690 --> 00:17:22.730
and kids in America felt the same way. Everyone
was mesmerized by the massive scale of this

00:17:22.730 --> 00:17:27.680
circus, and they only continued to improve
upon the show as time went on. The Barnum

00:17:27.680 --> 00:17:32.710
and Bailey Circus continued to tour long after
his death, only ending in 2017. It went on

00:17:32.710 --> 00:17:42.790
for 146 years.
Later Life, and Legacy

00:17:42.790 --> 00:17:48.430
As he grew older, fewer and fewer people criticized
PT Barnum for the tricks he played on audiences,

00:17:48.430 --> 00:17:53.840
and more people recognized the impact he made
on the entertainment industry. He was well-respect

00:17:53.840 --> 00:17:58.220
by just about everyone. He wrote four best-selling
books in his later years, including one called

00:17:58.220 --> 00:18:02.590
The Art of Money-Getting. In another book,
Humbugs of the World, he was very candid about

00:18:02.590 --> 00:18:07.460
psychics and magicians’ tricks, and he even
called out Harry Houdini. He knew all the

00:18:07.460 --> 00:18:12.800
tricks of the trade, and wrote that he would
pay $500 to any medium who could truly convince

00:18:12.800 --> 00:18:17.720
him that they were actually talking to the
dead. His point was that even though we know

00:18:17.720 --> 00:18:22.990
magic and ghosts are not real, that doesn’t
mean we can’t enjoy the spectacle.

00:18:22.990 --> 00:18:28.620
Very few people know that PT Barnum took politics
very seriously, especially in his later years.

00:18:28.620 --> 00:18:33.520
In his autobiography, he wrote that “a man
who takes no interest in politics is unfit

00:18:33.520 --> 00:18:39.140
to live in a land where the government rests
in the hands of the people.” In 1865, US

00:18:39.140 --> 00:18:43.890
Congress would be voting on the amendment
of the constitution to outlaw slavery. Technically,

00:18:43.890 --> 00:18:49.240
he had once been a slave-owner himself, with
Joice Heth. But he worked with and loved so

00:18:49.240 --> 00:18:53.060
many African American performers over the
years. He felt so passionately about fighting

00:18:53.060 --> 00:18:57.660
for their freedom, that he used his money
and connections to join the Republican party

00:18:57.660 --> 00:19:02.430
as part of the Connecticut legislature. He
wanted to do everything within his power to

00:19:02.430 --> 00:19:08.010
help end slavery in the United States, and
they succeeded. The time he spent on the Connecticut

00:19:08.010 --> 00:19:13.680
legislature wasn’t the end of his political
career. In 1875, he became the mayor of Bridgeport,

00:19:13.680 --> 00:19:15.630
Connecticut, and served for one year.

00:19:15.630 --> 00:19:19.690
When he was getting older, PT Barnum paid
a reporter from the local newspaper to write

00:19:19.690 --> 00:19:25.160
a very flattering eulogy so that he could
hear and enjoy it while he was still alive.

00:19:25.160 --> 00:19:31.230
He died in 1891 of a stroke, at 79 years old.
He loved his job so much, he was working up

00:19:31.230 --> 00:19:36.460
until the day he died, and his last words
were a request to look over some receipts.

00:19:36.460 --> 00:19:41.460
Two years after his death, a statue was built
in Seaside Park in Bridgeport, Connecticut,

00:19:41.460 --> 00:19:46.190
on land he had donated to the city.
Today, his legacy still lives on in the minds

00:19:46.190 --> 00:19:51.160
and hearts of everyone who remembers the circus,
and his work has influenced entertainment

00:19:51.160 --> 00:20:07.429
as we know it throughout 
the world.

